{"name":"Sparkling Brook","hex":"#dceee3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sparkling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of sparkle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, reflecting light as if giving off tiny sparks or flashes of light.","Of a beverage, especially an alcoholic beverage, containing dissolved carbon dioxide (either naturally or that has been added) that comes out of solution in the form of many tiny bubbles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sparkling"},{"term":"brook","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bear; endure; put up with; tolerate.","To enjoy the use of; make use of; profit by; to use, enjoy, possess, or hold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A body of running water smaller than a river; a small stream.","A water meadow, or  low, marshy ground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brook"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sparkling","blocks":[{"text":"Sparkling is an optical phenomenon exhibited by materials, both solids and liquids, in which numerous points of light appear and disappear, or change in brightness as the material, observer, or light source moves, creating an impression of brilliance and visual movement. In many materials, this effect results from specular reflection by small reflective particles, facets, or flakes that direct incident light toward the observer, although sparkling can also result from refraction and other optical phenomena."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Preferential_concentration.gif/330px-Preferential_concentration.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparkling"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Drinking_glass_00118.gif/250px-Drinking_glass_00118.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Drinking_glass_00118.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Drinking_glass_00118.gif/250px-Drinking_glass_00118.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bubbles create the sparkling effect in sparkling water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrinking_glass_00118.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg/500px-Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg/1280px-Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg/500px-Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sea otter surrounded by sun glitter, a natural form of sparkling","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sparkling":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sparkling.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sparkling.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sparkling.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sparkling.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sparkling.wav.ogg"}],"brook":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/En-us-brook.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=o